# 3. FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS

## 3.1 Multilingual voice capture

Implement real-time streaming speech-to-text.

Initial language targets:

- Kannada
- Hindi
- Tamil
- Telugu
- Malayalam
- Marathi
- Bengali
- Gujarati
- Punjabi
- Odia
- Assamese
- English

The architecture must allow additional Indian languages to be added through language-specific configurations.

The application must support natural language switching and English code-mixing within a single sentence.

Example:

"Today Nandini avarige ANC checkup madidvi, weight 52 kilo ide, TT injection complete agide."

The speech system must understand the sentence without requiring the worker to speak only English or only one language.

Recognise medical expressions such as:

- ANC checkup
- TT injection
- Td injection
- IFA goli
- Iron tablets
- Folic acid
- Pregnancy checkup
- Birth weight
- Newborn feeding
- Vaccination
- BCG
- OPV
- Pentavalent
- PHC
- ANM

Maintain a healthcare vocabulary dictionary with local pronunciations, spelling variants, synonyms and English aliases.

Do not automatically convert ambiguous medical terminology into another clinical term. Preserve the original spoken expression and request confirmation whenever necessary.

## 3.2 Streaming speech processing

Implement an end-to-end streaming voice pipeline.

Required features:

- Microphone audio streaming.
- Streaming STT.
- Partial transcript generation.
- Voice activity detection.
- Automatic endpointing.
- Noise suppression.
- Echo cancellation.
- Automatic gain control.
- Speaker interruption detection.
- Streaming TTS response.
- Audio playback cancellation.
- Conversation state management.

The application should start responding quickly after the worker finishes speaking.

Measure:

**Time to First Audio = First agent audio output timestamp − Caller speech endpoint timestamp.**

Engineering target:

- P50 target: under 1.2 seconds.
- P95 target: under 2 seconds.

These are performance goals, not assumed achievements. Measure and report actual results during testing.

## 3.3 Background noise handling

The system must be tested with:

- Normal household noise.
- Road traffic.
- Children crying.
- Multiple conversations.
- Fan noise.
- Outdoor wind.
- Low-volume speech.

Implement browser microphone noise suppression where available, followed by server-side or provider-side denoising when supported.

Do not assume that browser noise cancellation alone is sufficient.

Use VAD and endpointing to determine when the worker has completed a sentence.

Avoid cutting off the end of spoken numbers, names or dates.

If the transcript is unclear, ask the worker to repeat the particular information instead of guessing.

## 3.4 Structured visit record

After speech processing, extract information into a validated structured record.

Required common fields:

- Beneficiary ID.
- Beneficiary name.
- Household ID.
- Visit type.
- Visit date.
- ASHA worker ID.
- Spoken language.
- Visit observations.
- Weight, when reported.
- Measurements and units, when reported.
- Vaccination status.
- Vaccinations due.
- Next visit date.
- Follow-up requirements.
- Danger-sign status.
- Referral status.
- Source transcript.
- Field-level confidence.
- Confirmation status.

Support the following visit categories.

### A. Pregnancy / ANC visit

Capture:

- Beneficiary ID.
- Beneficiary name.
- Pregnancy identification.
- Gestational age or pregnancy week, when provided.
- ANC checkup status.
- Weight.
- Reported blood pressure, if provided.
- IFA tablet status.
- Td/TT vaccination information.
- Reported symptoms.
- Danger signs.
- Next visit date.
- Referral requirement.

Never invent a missing clinical measurement.

### B. Newborn visit

Capture:

- Newborn ID.
- Mother's beneficiary ID.
- Newborn name, when available.
- Date of birth.
- Age in days or weeks.
- Birth weight, when reported.
- Current weight, when reported.
- Feeding observations.
- Reported temperature.
- Immunisation status.
- Observed or reported danger signs.
- Next home visit date.
- Referral requirement.

### C. Immunisation visit

Capture:

- Beneficiary ID.
- Child's name.
- Date of birth.
- Age.
- Previous vaccine history, when provided.
- Vaccinations given.
- Vaccinations due.
- Missed vaccination information.
- Next immunisation date.
- Follow-up requirements.

Use a versioned, approved National Immunisation Schedule configuration. Do not invent vaccine recommendations or infer that a vaccine was administered merely because it was due.

